Day 6 – Firewalls, IDS/IPS & Network Defense

Today I explored how organizations monitor, detect, and defend against cyber threats in modern networks.

What I Learned
  How Firewalls monitor and control incoming and outgoing network traffic using predefined security rules.
  How IDS (Intrusion Detection Systems) detect suspicious or malicious network activities.
  How IPS (Intrusion Prevention Systems) actively block or prevent detected attacks in real time.
  The role of Honeypots in attracting attackers, collecting threat intelligence, and analyzing attack techniques.
  Why understanding both detection and evasion techniques helps defensive security teams build stronger security controls.
  Reinforced these concepts through lab-based demonstrations to understand real-world defense mechanisms.

  
Cybersecurity News
  WordPress patched CVE-2026-64638, a critical pre-authentication XSS vulnerability that researchers showed could be chained into PHP code execution if an administrator interacts with a malicious page.
  Linux fixed CVE-2026-64564 (SCTPhantom), an 18-year-old use-after-free vulnerability in SCTP that could allow local privilege escalation to root and even container escape.
  Researchers introduced NatJack, a new attack technique that manipulates NAT tables to hijack TCP sessions, spoof DNS responses, and disrupt network communications, emphasizing the importance of network segmentation and timely patching.

  
Key Takeaway

  Strong cybersecurity isn't just about blocking attacks—it requires continuous monitoring, detection, prevention, and learning from attacker behavior. Firewalls, IDS/IPS, Honeypots, and regular security updates work together to build a layered defense against evolving threats.

